      "expected": "LED anodes must be tied to a powered LED-supply net or driven source (for example +3V3 or the driven /LED_3.3V_POWER_NONINV net) so the LEDs can light when their cathode-side signals pull low.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"32\") (name \"/LED_3.3V_POWER_SW\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"D14\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D23\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D4\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"31\") (name \"/LED_3.3V_POWER_NONINV\") ... (node (ref \"U34\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") (pintype \"output\")) ..."}
      ],
      "impact": "The Ethernet link/activity LEDs D4, D14, and D23 are left with floating anodes and will not operate as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 is given schematic/SPICE value \"24k\", but its component description and MPN identify a 24Ω resistor.",
      "expected": "The reference, value, description, and MPN should agree. In this position across VOUTP/VOUTN, a 24k bleed/load resistor is consistent with the 0805 125 mW rating and the surrounding clamp network; a 24Ω part would be an excessive load.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\") ... (field (name \"Description\") \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\")"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the board is assembled from the MPN/description part (24Ω) instead of the schematic value (24k), VOUTP/VOUTN would be heavily loaded and the resistor could over-dissipate, changing the power/clamp behavior.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.5", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The GND2 pins of the isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 are connected directly to the system GND net, and their VCC2 supply is the non-isolated switched 5V CAN rail.",
      "expected": "For the intended isolated CAN buses, the transceiver secondary ground (GND2) and secondary supply should be isolated from the primary logic ground.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"80\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ... (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (`CAN0`, `CAN1`), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (`U8`, `U9`) through an isolated CAN transceiver (`U11`/companion)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN physical layer would not provide the intended galvanic isolation; the bus-side ground is tied to the board ground, defeating the isolated-CAN architecture and potentially introducing ground loops.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows at least three deviations from intended behavior: a floating LED-power net leaving D4/D14/D23 inoperative, a conflicting R36 value/MPN/description mismatch, and isolated CAN transceiver secondary grounds tied to system GND despite the specification calling for isolated CAN buses."
}

